<reference_puzzle index="1" source="train" puzzle_id="cc9053aa" lines="59-72">
<description>
Recolor the unique safe route between the two maroon endpoints from teal to maroon.

Input structure
- A black background contains a teal 4-connected track, exactly two maroon endpoint pixels, and one or more orange blocker pixels embedded near the track.

Rule
- Treat each orange blocker and its 8-neighborhood as forbidden.
- In the remaining teal/maroon track graph, there is exactly one simple
  4-connected path between the maroon endpoints.
- Recolor teal pixels on that path to maroon; leave endpoints, blockers, and all other cells unchanged.

Key insight
- The orange pixels block nearby teal cells diagonally as well as orthogonally, which removes shorter decoy routes and makes the intended endpoint-to-endpoint path unique.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="981add89" lines="10-29">
<description>
Extend each top-row control pixel downward as a vertical toggle ray in its own color.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Non-background pixels on the top row are control pixels, each identifying
  one column and one ray color.
- Foreground regions below may intersect these control columns.

Rule:
- For every top-row control pixel, process the cells below it in the same
  column down to the bottom edge.
- A cell that already has the control color is erased to the background.
- Every other cell on that downward ray is painted with the control color.
- The top-row control pixels themselves remain unchanged.

Key insight:
The top row is a set of vertical ray launchers; each ray toggles only cells
matching its own color and overwrites all other colors along that column.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="80214e03" lines="48-64">
<description>
Extract the color of each noisy block in a row/column lattice and mirror the coarse grid horizontally.

Input structure:
- A background surrounds a rectangular lattice of monochrome foreground blocks.
- Consecutive rows in a lattice band share the same set of visible foreground colors.
- Consecutive columns in a lattice band share the same set of visible foreground colors.
- Each row-band/column-band intersection has one foreground color, with possible background holes.

Rule:
- Group non-empty rows and columns into consecutive bands by their foreground color sets.
- Replace each lattice cell by the single foreground color inside that row/column-band intersection.
- Return the resulting coarse color grid with the column order reversed.

Key insight:
- Whole-row and whole-column color sets reveal the hidden lattice even when neighboring cells share a color or contain holes.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="42f83767" lines="23-39">
<description>
Expand the lower color grid by replacing each pixel with its matching top stencil.

Input structure
The input has a top legend and a lower dense color grid, separated by blank rows.
The legend contains left-to-right 2x2 color swatches and, to their right,
the same number of square neutral-color stencil masks.

Rule
Pair the swatches and stencil masks by left-to-right order. For every pixel in
the lower color grid, paste the paired stencil mask as one output block,
recoloring the mask pixels with that pixel's color and leaving mask holes as bg.

Key insight
The lower grid is not copied directly; it is an index grid whose colors select
the stencil shape from the legend, so each input pixel becomes one square block.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="eval" puzzle_id="35ab12c3" lines="73-88">
<description>
Complete outlined shapes and carry attached shadow pixels along their edges.

Input structure:
- bg = background. Each active color marks vertices of an 8-direction aligned outline.
- A start vertex can have adjacent non-bg, different-colored shadow pixels.
- Other colored pixels may be distractors unless they lie on valid drawable edges.

Rule:
- For each color, order its pixels by polar angle around their centroid to form a cycle.
- Draw only horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree edges whose interiors are clear.
- From the chosen start vertex, copy adjacent shadow offsets along each drawable edge
  when the parallel path is in bounds and clear.

Key insight: The main outline is reconstructed first, then the start vertex's neighbor offsets define parallel shadow strokes.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="e84fef15" lines="39-59">
<description>
Reconstruct the repeated tile from its separated copies and mark the missing pixels in the damaged copy blue.

Input structure:
- The input is a lattice of equal-size blocks separated by one-cell solid rows
  and columns of the same separator color.
- All blocks are copies of one motif except exactly one block, where some
  motif pixels have been overwritten by the motif background color.
- Blue is absent from the input and is reserved as the output marker color.

Rule:
- Recover the motif by taking the unique majority color at each local block
  coordinate across all block copies.
- Find the single block that differs from this consensus motif.
- Return the consensus motif, recoloring the differing local coordinates blue.

Key insight:
- The repeated copies provide redundancy: the damaged positions are exactly
  the coordinates where the lone nonconforming copy has background instead of
  the consensus motif color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="b5bb5719" lines="17-32">
<description>
Fill the background under a top-row red/grey span with a shrinking diagonal pattern that alternates red and grey.

Input structure:
- The grid has an orange background.
- All foreground pixels are red or grey and lie on the top row.
- The first and last top-row foreground pixels define an odd-width span.

Rule:
- Copy the input grid.
- For row r below the top, fill columns span_left + r through span_right - r.
- Each filled cell takes the top-row color r columns up-left, flipped red<->grey once per row step.

Key insight:
- The output is the top-row span propagated down-right along diagonals, while the allowable region shrinks inward from both ends each row.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="eval" puzzle_id="38007db0" lines="9-22">
<description>
Extract the unique cell from each lattice row into a single-column lattice.

Input structure:
- Grid divided into equal square cells by uniform gridlines of color L.
- Cell interiors filled with bg; each cell has an inner sprite pattern.
- Each row of cells has exactly one cell with a unique pattern; the rest are copies.

Rule:
- For each lattice row, find the cell whose inner pattern appears exactly once (the "odd one out").
- Construct a single-column lattice preserving the original row heights, containing only the unique cells.

Key insight: The task is per-row "odd one out" — every cell in a row is identical except one oddball, which must be extracted.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="7b5033c1" lines="7-21">
<description>
Straighten a colored snake path into a single-column strip preserving pixel order.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Non-bg pixels form a single 4-connected path (snake) with exactly two endpoints.
- Each path pixel may be a different non-bg color.

Rule:
- Find the two endpoints (cells with only one path neighbor) of the snake.
- Begin at the top-left-most endpoint and trace the path cell by cell.
- Output an (N×1) column listing each cell's color in traversal order.

Key insight: The path is a simple chain with no branches; choosing the top-left-most endpoint fixes a unique reading direction.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="14b8e18c" lines="40-57">
<description>
Mark every complete square object with red pixels just outside its corners.

Input structure:
- A uniform background contains one or more monochrome 4-connected foreground objects.
- The relevant objects are square frames or filled squares: their bbox is square
  and all cells on the bbox border have the object's color.
- Non-square bars and incomplete fragments may also appear and are ignored.

Rule:
- Keep the input unchanged.
- For each complete square object, paint red on the two outside background
  pixels orthogonally adjacent to each of the square's four corners.

Key insight: The transformation is not based on size or color; it selects
objects whose bounding box is a complete square border and labels only their
exposed outer corner-adjacent cells.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="cf5fd0ad" lines="10-22">
<description>
Build a four-quadrant rotational tiling of the input square.

Input structure
- The input is a square grid treated as one complete sprite.

Rule
- Each output quadrant is a 2x2 repetition of a rotated copy of the whole input.
- The quadrant rotations are upper-left 180 degrees, upper-right 90 degrees
  clockwise, lower-left 90 degrees counterclockwise, and lower-right unchanged.

Key insight
- The output is a 4x4 grid of original-size blocks, not a per-pixel scale-up.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

